ush players to spend higher cards. I have the Ace triplet saved as a control card.\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\n{"cards": ["♠4", "♥10", "♣10", "♦10"]}\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The hand possesses strong control cards (Rocket, Pair of 2s, Triplet of Aces) but suffers from a lack of connectivity in the lower ranks (singles 4, 5, 6, 8 and pair 33). There are no straights available. The most efficient strategy to clear the small junk cards is to utilize the "Triplet with Single" pattern. I have two triplets (10s and As). Playing the 10s first is safer to provoke opponents\' high cards while keeping the Aces for later. Attaching the single 4 is optimal because it is the lowest isolated single; breaking the pair of 3s would result in two low singles, increasing the number of turns needed to empty the hand.\n\nReason: To maximize hand efficiency by clearing a low single (4) along with a mid-tier triplet (10s), preserving the high-value triplet (As) and the pair structure (33) for later plays.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠4", "♥10", "♣10", "♦10"]}'}
