nd the pair of 2s. The King *forces* a response or, ideally, allows me to lead with a weaker card afterwards, thereby controlling the pace of the game.\n\nUltimately, I am clearing my hand and forcing the opponent to use resources. So, the play is clear. I'm playing the King. It is the best move to achieve my long-term strategy of controlling the game and clearing my hand.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The hand contains a Rocket (Dual Jokers) and a pair of 2s, which provides absolute control over the game. The current hand composition consists mostly of singles (6, 8, Q, K) and pairs (99, 22). The goal is to clear the singles efficiently. The opponent played a single Q. Playing the King is the standard move: it beats the Q, forces out the remaining Ace or a 2 from the opponents, and preserves the powerful pair of 2s for later control. Passing would allow the Right opponent to lead freely, which is unnecessary given the strength of the Landlord\'s hand. Breaking the pair of 2s is not needed to beat a Queen.\n\nReason: You have a very strong hand with the Rocket and a pair of 2s. To win, you need to clear your singles (6, 8, Q, K). Playing the King beats the current Queen, potentially forcing an opponent to spend a top card (Ace or 2) or allowing you to regain the lead to play your lower cards (like 99 or single 6/8). There is only one Ace left unknown, making the King a very strong play.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♣K"]}'}
